nof schemes completely changed the way people did algebraic\ngeometry.Basic Global Relative\nInvariants for\nHomogeneous Linear\nDifferential\nEquationsThis remarkable volume contains a large part of the mathe-\nmatical correspondence between A. Grothendieck and J.-P.\nSerre. This correspondence forms a vivid introduction to the\nstudy of algebraic geometry during the years 1955–1965. For\nexample, readers will discover the genesis of some of Grothen-\ndieck’s ideas on Sheaf cohomology, schemes, Riemann-Roch,\nthe fundamental group, motives, and more. They also will get\nan idea of the mathematical athmosphere of this time (Bour-\nbaki, seminars in Paris, Harvard, Princeton, war in Algeria,\netc.). This is a remarkable book.Roger Chalkley, University of  \nCincinnati, Cincinnati, OHContents: Introduction; Some problems of historical importance;Illustrations for some results in chapters 1 and 2; \\(L_n\\) and \\(I_{n,i}\\)\nas semi-invariants of the first kind; \\(V_n\\) and \\(J_{n,i}\\) as semi-invari-This item will also be of interest to those working in algebra\nand algebraic geometry.VOLUME 49, NUMBER 3362NOTICES OF THE AMS\n\nMemoirs of the American Mathematical Society cover page with title “Smooth Molecular Decompositions of Functions and Singular Integral Operators” and authors listed (J. E. Gilbert, Y. S. Han, J. A. Hogen, J. D. Lagkey, D. Welland, G. Weiss), plus volume/number and American Mathematical Society branding.\n\nA grayscale book cover image: “DOCUMENTS MATHÉMATIQUES” at the top, title “CORRESPONDANCE GROTHENDIECK-SERRE” with authors “Pierre Colmez, Jean-Pierre Serre” and a small illustration/graphic near the bottom; “SOCIÉTÉ MATHÉMATIQUE DE FRANCE” at the bottom.\n\nA book/journal cover page: “MEMOIRS of the American Mathematical Society” at the top, with “Volume 156 Number 744” and the title “Basic Global Relative Invariants for Homogeneous Linear Differential Equations” by “Roger Chalkley,” plus an AMS logo and “American Mathematical Society” 